with around 50 000 rail workers going on  strike from tomorrow we're getting  warnings this morning that strikes may  cross over excuse me to teachers to  healthcare staff and airline staff as  well but how realistic a prospect is a  summer of discontent let's speak now to  mark zucker the uh general secretary of  one of the uk's largest trade unions the  public and commercial services union hi  good to see you thanks for joining us  summer of discontent what do you think  well i represent civil servants and  we've taken the decision that we are  balloting 150 000 civil servants for  industrial action in september  i think the national education union are  talking about balloting in the autumn as  well so i'm not sure about a summer of  discontent clearly we've got ray workers  on strike this week but what i am sure  about is that we will see high levels of  industrial action unless the government  recognize that frontline public sector  workers who kept the country running  during the pandemic  cannot be expected to have a two percent  pay raise when inflation is forecast to  be over 11 that is a historic cutting  living standards people cannot afford it  and therefore the government needs to do  something about it if they don't then i  do believe we are in for quite a lot of  industrial action  the government and indeed the governor  of the bank of england both saying that  um workers need to be realistic about  what they're expecting  otherwise the inflation will continue to  spiral  yes i i notice it's often rich people  tell those who haven't got much that  they've always got to be realistic  but what the government knows if you  take their own workforce is we have had  11 years  of pay cuts in real terms already so on  top of 11 years of pay cuts when the  average wage of the people i represent  is a fraction under 24 000 pounds a year  when tens of thousands of civil servants  have to claim in-work benefits because  their pay is already so low saying be  realistic just doesn't cut it because it  doesn't pay your fuel bill it doesn't  pay your council tax hike it doesn't  help you put food on the table only a  real terms pay raise does that and the  government must know that and the  governor of the bank of england must  know that so they just need to accept  that in this crisis those people who've  done so much to keep the country running  deserve to have a decent pay raise not  to take a further cut in their living  standards even if it feels uh the spiral  of inflation  well we're often told this wage is  fueling the spiral of inflation but at  the moment we know that's not the case  wages have nothing to do with the  current massive run on inflation and of  course if we think about it telling  workers that they are the ones who can  never have a pay raise really tells  people that the only way we can fight  inflation is that those who are  suffering most must take more pain and  frankly i saw your piece earlier okay it  was a fantastic piece if you are on  benefits or if you're on in work  benefits or if you are struggling  already to make ends meet  it cannot be right  that a train worker or a job center  worker or a refuge collector already  struggling haven't had a holiday for  years worrying about paying the bills  already i told you must pay the price to  keep inflation under control it's just  not acceptable and i don't think people  will accept it and i believe most  members of the public  will support frontline public sector  workers getting a decent pay raise but  but do you acknowledge um what the  governor of the bank of england is  saying because presumably he's in that  job because he knows what he's talking  about and unless we moderate our demands  for a pay increase that is lower than  inflation it will just continue to  spiral and make the problem worse  well it's it's easy for the government  bankruptcy to say that and i don't  frankly accept that i mean inflation  they're forecasting probably over the  next year or two will get back under  control but i think it's for the  government to find out ways of trying to  get inflation under control that doesn't  mean the people paying that terrible  price are teachers and nurses and job  center workers and the people who are  already struggling i mean i i'm often  told by people well if you go on strike  for more pay it means the price of food  goes up in the shops but the reality is  that the people we represent already are  struggling to pay for the things in the  shops and if they get a pay raise they  can manage their household bills in the  way that they choose but it can't be  right that the people with the least  are always told that they have to pay  the biggest price that's not fair and i  don't think people will accept it  do you think the rail strikes will go  ahead this week  well i'm not in the rmt and i'm not on  their negotiating team but it seems to  me all the mood music is that they will  go ahead and the thing that i would ask  viewers to just take on board is i've  heard grant shaps who i think is a  shocking secretary of state for  transport i've been unfortunate enough  to have to deal with him over our  dispute in the dvla last year he's a  dreadful minister  but i think that most people will accept  that that disruption is not down to the  fact that workers will now lose pay to  withdraw their labor it is down to the  fact that the government is allowing the  strike to go ahead instead of  intervening to give job security for  workers who worry about the future and  give our a workers a decent pay rise and  i for one  would be happily to be inconvenienced if  that inconvenience means that our  frontline rate workers men and women who  do such a fantastic job and work so hard  in the pandemic  that those people get a real living wage  increase and they get job security  um just before i let you go we did have  simon hart uh the chief secretary to the  treasury simon clark for give me on the  programme earlier today and i asked him  whether rail workers um have to suffer a  cut in pay he says we can't have  inflation busting pay increases the um  we tweeted that out the rmt union has  just retweeted that and said unless of  course it's for ceos because those  private school fields won't pay  themselves he added uh making uh light  uh or certainly um sneering at what the  government minister had to say is that  something is that an approach that you  would endorse  well i think there's a lot of hypocrisy  that is spoken by the government and to  be honest when there's an onslaught  demonizing women and men frontline  workers struggling so hard i think it's  entirely reasonable that their union  seeks to have their back and of course  it is true  that the people invariably telling those  with the least to tighten their belts  more are the ones who are incredibly  rich and we only have to look at our  chancellor the richest man in parliament  who is happy to tell people that they've  got to tighten their belts but he  himself doesn't know what it's like to  tighten his belt so if unions are  speaking it on behalf of their members i  back them and i back their dispute  okay good to talk to you thanks for  joining us on the program thank you  thank you
 

